WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:02.820
INTRO:
Recently, cryptocurrencies haven’t had the

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:04.570
best of times.

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:10.840
Bitcoin and Ethereum are down nearly 50% while
many altcoins are down 70 to 80%.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.450
And this makes sense given that interest in
Bitcoin, NFTs, and crypto in general have

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.520
fallen off substantially.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.189
During such downturns, one of the most desirable
positions within the market is being the person

00:00:22.189 --> 00:00:23.739
who’s selling the shovels.

00:00:23.739 --> 00:00:28.060
Of course, crypto brokerages aren’t making
nearly as much money as they used to given

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:32.270
lower trading volume, and their own stocks
have been hammered by the market.

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:37.660
Coinbase, for example, is down nearly 70%
from their all time high.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:42.250
But despite this, Coinbase should still be
profitable meaning that they’re able to

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.530
turn a profit in any sort of market conditions.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:50.550
And as you would guess, the guy behind Coinbase,
Brian Armstrong, is still doing quite well

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.629
with a net worth of just over $5 billion.

00:00:53.629 --> 00:00:58.620
Ironically, Brian’s net worth stood at $16
billion just 12 months ago.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.260
This means that on average net worth over
the past year is about $10 billion depending

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.990
on how the crypto markets are performing.

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:09.270
Coinbase, however, hasn’t even been a thing
for 10 years at this point.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:16.490
So, here’s how Brian Armstrong made $10
billion in 10 years.

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:20.240
PRE-COINBASE:
Taking a look back, Brian’s story dates

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.280
back to January 25, 1983 to San Jose, California.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.820
Brian was born to a pretty well off family
of engineers, and from a very young age, he

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:31.220
had a pretty strong inclination towards technology.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:35.920
Nowaday’s this is nothing special given
that this basically applies to all of us,

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:41.560
but back in the 1980s and 90s, average people
were just starting to buy their first computers.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:45.220
During high school, it looks like Brian was
the classic computer geek who chose to learn

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.910
Java and CSS during his freetime.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:51.500
Brian recalls that getting strange looks from
his peers due to his quote “nerdiness”

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.210
was extremely common.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:56.490
Weird looks weren’t the only thing that
his coding skills got him though.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:00.230
Brian was able to leverage his skills to design
websites and programs for local businesses

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.640
and earn some nice money on the side.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.300
Soon enough, Brian would graduate from high
school in 2001 and he would attend Rice University

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:10.470
where he double majored in Computer Science
and Economics.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:14.400
While he likely performed great in school,
he never put all of his eggs in one basket.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.760
During college, he opened up a website that
could match tutors with students called universitytutor.com.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.670
The service wasn’t anything particularly
special, but Brian was rather early to the

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:28.080
game having launched this service in June
of 2003, and he ran the business quite well.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.600
So he was able to capture quite a bit of marketshare
and this would turn into a full time job over

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:33.810
the next several years.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.750
Brian didn’t drop out of college either
though.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:40.700
Instead he would actually go onto complete
a masters in computer science at Rice University

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.810
right after he finished his bachelors.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:47.250
Brian also got an internship at IBM where
he helped them create some Java based tools

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.590
that could be used in conjunction with network
attached storage devices.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:55.280
Anyway, shortly after finishing college, Brian
would decide to take a one year trip to Buenos

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.860
Aires, Argentina, and this trip would turn
out to be extremely influential.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:04.530
You see, Argentina has been struggling with
a chronic inflation problem for decades.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:08.550
In 2004, you could only get 2.81 Argentine
Pesos with one dollar.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:15.250
Today, however, you can get 114.39 Aregentine
pesos meaning that the currency is worth 40

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:17.350
times less relative to the dollar.

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:21.430
Living in Buenos Aires, Brian was witnessing
this crisis firsthand, and this didn’t give

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:24.490
him a particularly positive view towards fiat
currency.

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:29.920
After Brian returned to the US, he continued
serving as the CEO of universitytutor.com.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.750
But I don’t think you’d be surprised to
hear that Brian was also constantly on the

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:35.680
look out for new opportunities.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.040
One of these opportunities was working at
Airbnb as a software engineer.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:45.040
At the time, Airbnb was operating in 190 countries
and Brian had a chance to work on their international

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:46.180
payment systems.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:50.080
This experience made him realize just how
difficult it was to send and receive money

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.060
in certain developing countries.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:55.910
And this would give him the final push he
needed to go all in on Coinbase.

00:03:55.910 --> 00:04:02.400
FOUNDING COINBASE:
While it wasn’t till mid 2012 that Brian

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.740
left UnversityTutor and went all in on Coinbase,
by this point, he had already been working

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.660
on the idea for a few years in the background.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:15.180
Brian first heard about Bitcoin in December
of 2010 when he came across Bitcoin’s white

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.209
paper.

00:04:16.209 --> 00:04:20.459
Being a serial entrepreneur since high school,
Brian instantly knew that he had to try something

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:24.460
in this space, but he wasn’t sure about
what exactly he should do.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:28.409
Initially, it seems that Brian was interested
in creating something like Venmo or Square

00:04:28.409 --> 00:04:29.610
but for Bitcoin.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:33.830
However, he quickly realized that Bitcoin
wasn’t that great when it came to transactions

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:35.080
due to it’s high fees.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:40.490
So, instead, he decided to just focus on helping
people buy and hold crypto and this of course

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:43.289
led him down the crypto brokerage route.

00:04:43.289 --> 00:04:46.930
Over the next two years, Brian would spend
all of his free time writing code that would

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.860
allow users to buy and store various cryptocurrencies.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:53.530
Brian also spent a lot of his time on the
Bitcoin subreddit to better understand what

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:57.930
crypto enthusiasts were looking for, and this
is when Brian would run into a man named Fred

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:58.930
Ehrsam.

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:02.900
Fred had a classic wall street job as a foreign
exchange trader at Goldman Sachs.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:07.509
In his freetime, he liked to trade Bitcoin,
and this is what led him to the Bitcoin subreddit.

00:05:07.509 --> 00:05:11.900
As you might’ve guessed, Brian and Fred
would quickly become friends and Fred would

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:13.790
join Coinbase as a cofounder.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:19.189
The duo was also able to secure $150,000 worth
of startup capital from Y Combinator which

00:05:19.189 --> 00:05:21.969
helped tremendously with initial startup expenses.

00:05:21.969 --> 00:05:25.620
And with that the duo launched Coinbase on
June 20, 2012.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:29.930
At the time, coinbase was one of the only
choices you had if you wanted to conveniently

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:31.830
buy bitcoin within the US.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:36.699
So, naturally, all of the original crypto
enthusiasts flocked over the Coinbase, Kraken,

00:05:36.699 --> 00:05:37.699
and Bitstamp.

00:05:37.699 --> 00:05:41.710
For many of these guys, the brokerage didn’t
matter all that much given that they had no

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:44.360
plans of keeping their coins on the brokerage.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:48.150
Most of these guys instantly transferred their
coins into their Bitcoin wallets and eventually

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:49.949
into cold storage options.

00:05:49.949 --> 00:05:53.680
Nonetheless, Coinbase was able to make quite
a bit of money with each bitcoin purchase

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.529
or sale thanks to their hefty commission fees.

00:05:56.529 --> 00:06:01.229
Even today, their fees are rather high especially
in comparison to the stock market.

00:06:01.229 --> 00:06:07.809
For example, if you buy $10,000 worth of crypto,
you have to pay Coinbase between $40 and $60.

00:06:07.809 --> 00:06:11.710
Despite these fees, Coinbase has never really
struggled with a lack of interest given that

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:17.099
crypto returns are so high that these fees
often seem insignificant in comparison.

00:06:17.099 --> 00:06:22.120
Within just the first two years of launching,
Coinbase was able to grow to 1 million users.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.339
And this exponential growth made it extremely
easy to raise money.

00:06:25.339 --> 00:06:30.309
In May of 2013, Union Square Ventures invested
$5 million in Coinbase.

00:06:30.309 --> 00:06:34.719
At the time, this seemed like an extremely
risky move given that this was the biggest

00:06:34.719 --> 00:06:36.999
funding round in Bitcoin startup history.

00:06:36.999 --> 00:06:42.629
But, knowing that this stake would eventually
grow to $4.6 billion, I think it worked out

00:06:42.629 --> 00:06:44.090
just fine.

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:48.610
Just a couple of months after this, Coinbase
would raise another $25 million, and with

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:50.870
that, they were off to the races.

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:56.550
BUILDING AN EMPIRE:
Using all of this new found capital, Brian

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:59.439
would continue making Coinbase more secure.

00:06:59.439 --> 00:07:05.189
For example, in 2014, Brian purchased insurance
for all of the Bitcoin stored on Coinbase

00:07:05.189 --> 00:07:06.189
servers.

00:07:06.189 --> 00:07:09.169
Similarly, Coinbase also launched their vault
system which is supposed to offer extremely

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:10.860
secure bitcoin storage.

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:15.250
Such initiatives made Coinbase customers feel
a lot more comfortable and many of them started

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:18.379
to hold more crypto inside Coinbase itself.

00:07:18.379 --> 00:07:22.860
Aside from upping the reliability of coinbase,
Brian also worked on expanding their features.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:28.279
One of their biggest launches came in 2015
when the company came out with Coinbase Exchange.

00:07:28.279 --> 00:07:32.569
This exchange was more geared towards crypto
traders and enthusiasts and boasted significantly

00:07:32.569 --> 00:07:35.110
lower fees and bid ask spreads.

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:39.189
It also came along with more sophisticated
charts, indicators, and limit orders.

00:07:39.189 --> 00:07:44.229
Yet, I don’t think Coinbase Exchange was
ever able to reach its full potential.

00:07:44.229 --> 00:07:46.659
First of all, they had a branding crisis for
a few years.

00:07:46.659 --> 00:07:51.099
Initially, they were renamed Global Digital
Asset Exchange before they were eventually

00:07:51.099 --> 00:07:53.080
renamed Coinbase Pro.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:57.439
Aside from this branding issue, i don’t
think Coinbase Pro actually ever reached its

00:07:57.439 --> 00:07:58.860
target market.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:03.249
Even though Coinbase Pro is an exceptional
service and quite popular, the vast majority

00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:08.210
of people who use Coinbase Pro are simply
crypto hodlers who want lower fees.

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:12.029
It seems like the high frequency traders far
prefer the offerings from Binance which is

00:08:12.029 --> 00:08:14.939
evident in their substantially higher trading
volume.

00:08:14.939 --> 00:08:18.500
Coinbase currently averages about $1 billion
per day in trading volume.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:22.779
Meanwhile, Binance averages nearly $30 billion
per day in trading volume.

00:08:22.779 --> 00:08:27.319
While Coinbase didn’t quite winover the
trading market, this experience allowed them

00:08:27.319 --> 00:08:31.429
to better understand their average customer
and position in the market.

00:08:31.429 --> 00:08:35.940
Coinbase isn’t the platform for traders,
it’s the platform for everyday crypto holders,

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:39.140
and this became extremely evident during the
2017 bull run.

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:44.030
In the last quarter of 2017, Coinbase’s
user count nearly doubled in a matter of months.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:48.540
We saw a similar growth percentage in the
first quarter of 2021 as well.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:52.449
Brian has since chosen to lean into the casual
demographic and launch features that would

00:08:52.449 --> 00:08:53.449
appeal to them.

00:08:53.449 --> 00:08:56.500
For example, in October of 2020, Coinbase
launched, Coinbase worked with Visa to launch

00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:01.020
their own debit card which apparently offers
up to 4% back in rewards.

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:05.220
And more recently, Coinbase ran an extremely
successful ad at the 2022 superbowl.

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.899
The ad wasn’t anything super flashy or anything.

00:09:07.899 --> 00:09:12.339
It was literally just a QR code that was bouncing
off the walls of the screen.

00:09:12.339 --> 00:09:16.370
However, given that it garnered 20 million
hits in one minute, crashed the entire Coinbase

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:21.110
website, and caused Coinbase to jump from
186 in the app store to number 2, I think

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:24.941
it’s safe to say that the ad was a roaring
success and that brings us to where we are

00:09:24.941 --> 00:09:26.690
today.

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:31.691
COINBASE TODAY:
Likely the biggest event for Coinbase aside

00:09:31.691 --> 00:09:35.540
from the Superbowl ad in recent history is
their IPO.

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:41.220
Unfortunately, unlike their superbowl ad,
their IPO wasn’t a roaring success.

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:45.579
The IPO was actually extremely hyped, and
people bought in signifcnatly on the day of

00:09:45.579 --> 00:09:46.579
the IPO.

00:09:46.579 --> 00:09:48.920
But, coinbase has sold off eversince.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:53.740
Also, something that we should note is that
the peak of Bitcoin was literally on the exact

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:55.480
day that Coinbase IPOd.

00:09:55.480 --> 00:10:00.769
Bitcoin did go onto make a new all time high
last November, but this was new peak was only

00:10:00.769 --> 00:10:05.160
marginally higher than the old peak, and I
think the general consensus is that the April

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:07.300
peak was the more significant peak.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:11.750
So, Coinbase’s IPO was literally the peak
of the crypto market.

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:16.230
This is quite ironic given that Coinbase usually
has the opposite effect on crypto.

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:20.839
Whenever a crypto is listed on Coinbase, it
often surges given that a lot more people

00:10:20.839 --> 00:10:22.880
are able to buy the given crypto.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:27.220
But I guess Coinbase themselves being listed
was bad luck for the crypto market at least

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:28.380
in the short term.

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:32.560
Looking forward, given that crypto is still
only in it’s infancy, it’s likely that

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:36.310
Coinbase has significant growth left both
in terms of their total user count and the

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:38.440
average holdings per user.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:42.810
And at this rate, I think that Brian is on
track to hit the $100 billion mark within

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:45.550
the next 10 to 20 years if not sooner.

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:49.720
Fortunately, Brian is trying his best to put
all this wealth to good use.

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:54.610
A few years ago, he founded givecrypto.com
which is a nonprofit that distributes cryptocurrency

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:56.009
to people living in poverty.

00:10:56.009 --> 00:10:59.800
He has also signed the giving pledge which
means that he has pledged to give away the

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:01.970
vast majority of his wealth.

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:05.600
Considering such wholesome ambitions, I believe
he deserves all the success he’s achieved

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:08.390
so far, but that’s just what I think.

